course: money
lesson: 6
title: Pocket money and earning it with chores
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Pocket money, also called an allowance, is a small regular amount that parents give children so they can learn to handle money themselves.
explain: Ella gets £3 every Saturday and spends it all on six 50p bags of sweets by Sunday night, so the rest of the week she has nothing; a plan for your money is called a budget.
explain: Some families pay for extra jobs, like washing the car, and agreeing the price before you start is called negotiating; other jobs, like making your own bed, are just part of living in a family.
explain: Families have different amounts of money and different rules, so one friend's £10 and another's £2 are both normal, and neither child is better than the other.
fact: Pocket money (an allowance) is regular money parents give children to learn how to handle money.
fact: A budget is a plan for how you will use your money.
fact: Chores are jobs around the house; some families pay for extra chores and some do not.
fact: To negotiate is to talk until both people agree a deal, such as a price for a job.
fact: Families have different incomes and rules; some pocket money must cover more things, like lunch, so comparing only the number tells you little.
fact: Some families give pocket money only if certain jobs are done, the way grown-ups are paid only for work they do.
fact: Being reliable means doing a job every time you promised, even on rainy days.
fact: Pocket money paid into a banking app is real money, just kept as numbers instead of coins.
fact: Taking money from a parent's purse without asking is stealing, even in a family, and it breaks trust.
fact: Some months have 4 weeks and some have 5, so money paid weekly does not always add up to the same as money paid monthly.
fact: Sixpence was an old British coin; two sixpences made a shilling, and prices were much smaller then.
fact: A windfall is money you did not expect, like a gift from Gran.
exercise: What is pocket money? | Regular money parents give children to learn about money | Money children must pay their parents each week | Money the school gives for good marks | Money found in coat pockets
exercise: Ella gets £3 and buys sweets at 50p a bag. How many bags can £3 buy? | 6 | 3 | 5 | 8
exercise: Josh and Dad agree £3 for each car wash. How much does Josh earn for 4 washes? | £12 | £7 | £15 | £10
exercise: What is it called when Josh and Dad talk until they agree a price? | Negotiating | Borrowing | Saving | Budgeting
exercise: Ruby gets £2 a week and her friend gets £10. What is the difference? | £8 | £12 | £5 | £6
exercise: Why can't Ruby just say her friend is "luckier"? | Her friend may have to buy her own lunch from it, and families differ | Her friend is better at chores | Ruby's family is doing something wrong | £2 is always worth more than £10
exercise: Dylan normally gets £4 a week but only if his room is tidy. How much would 4 messy weeks cost him? | £16 | £4 | £8 | £12
exercise: Aoife's mum won't pay for making her own bed. Why? | Some jobs are just part of living in a family | Making beds is far too easy to count as a proper job | Mum has no money left at all after the weekly shop | Beds are against the rules of the pocket money deal
exercise: Leo walks a dog 5 times a week for £1 a walk. How much does he earn in 4 weeks? | £20 | £5 | £9 | £25
exercise: What does it mean to be reliable? | Doing the job every time you promised | Doing the job only when it is sunny and you feel like it | Asking for a little more money each week for the same job | Letting a brother or sister do it and keeping the money
exercise: Maya gets £2 a week in her app. How much after 10 weeks? | £20 | £12 | £10 | £22
exercise: Callum's mate says "nick a quid from your mum's purse". Why say no? | It is stealing and breaks trust, even in a family | It is fine as long as she doesn't notice | A pound is too small to matter | Mums always give it back anyway
exercise: Nora can get £3 a week. In a month with 5 weeks, how much is that? | £15 | £12 | £8 | £18
exercise: Jayden had £6, then Gran gave him a tenner. How much does he have now? | £16 | £10 | £60 | £4
exercise: What is a budget? | A plan for how to use your money | A special type of piggy bank with a lock | Money borrowed from a friend until Friday | The price of a big toy in the shop window
exercise: What did two sixpences make in old British money? | A shilling | A half-crown | A guinea coin | A pound note
test: Ella wails "where'd all my money go?!" on Sunday night. What would help next week? | A budget: plan to keep some for later in the week | Asking Mum for double money so it lasts the whole week | Spending it all even faster | Hiding the sweets from herself
test: Josh: "Make it a fiver, it's proper mucky." Dad: "Three, and you do the wheels." ("Mucky" means dirty.) They shake on £3. What did they just do? | Negotiate a price before the job | Argue loudly and never make any deal | Borrow money from each other to pay | Swap the car for a bike to make it fair
test: Ruby moans "it's well unfair, she gets a tenner and I get two quid!" What's the kindest true answer? | Families have different money and rules, and that's normal | Ruby's family should be ashamed | Her friend must be doing more chores | £2 is enough for everything anyway
test: Leo's like "easy money, bruv" about dog walks — then it rains all week. What does a reliable walker do? | Keeps walking the dog as promised | Stops until the sun comes out | Asks for £10 a walk because of rain | Lets the dog walk itself
test: Tom's gran: "I got sixpence and thought I was the Queen of Sheba!" What does she mean? | She felt rich, because sixpence bought more back then | Sixpence was worth a million pounds | She was really a queen | Sixpence was a gold bar
test: Nora's dad offers £3 a week or £12 a month. Over a whole year (52 weeks), which gives more? | Weekly: £156 against £144 | Monthly: £144 against £156 | They give exactly the same | Monthly: £156 against £144
test: Callum's mate goes "she won't notice, don't be a wuss" ("wuss" means a coward). What's the best reply? | No, it's stealing and I'd lose her trust | Fine, but only a pound, so it doesn't really count | Only if you do it too | Okay, I'll pay it back sometime
test: A gran in Newcastle slips her grandson a tenner: "divvn't tell yer mam, pet" ("divvn't" means don't). What's a sensible thing to do? | Say thanks, and be honest with Mum about it | Hide it and never tell anyone | Spend it before anyone sees | Give it back because gifts of money are always wrong
test: Dylan's room "looks like a bomb hit it" on Friday, and the rule is tidy room = £4. What happens? | He gets no pocket money that week | He gets £4 anyway | He gets double pocket money next week instead | Mum tidies it and pays him
test: What would happen if Aoife got paid for every single little job, even brushing her teeth? | Helping at home would start to feel like a job only done for cash | She'd become a millionaire by Friday | Nothing, all families do this | Her teeth would get cleaner
test: Maya says app money "isn't real cos it's just numbers". Is she right? | No, it's real money kept in a bank as numbers | Yes, only coins are real | Yes, apps just print their own pretend money on screen | No, it's real only if it's gold
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises pocket money? | A regular sum furnished by parents to enable a child to learn the management of money | A sum payable by the child to the parents in respect of board and lodging | A remuneration paid by the school in respect of academic merit | A sum discovered within the pockets of garments
exam: It is given that a sum of three pounds is received weekly. Compute the aggregate received over a period of five weeks. | Fifteen pounds | Twelve pounds | Eight pounds | Eighteen pounds
exam: Identify the principal objection to the removal of money from a parent's purse without permission. | It constitutes theft and occasions a breach of trust | It constitutes a permissible loan within a family | It is objectionable solely where the sum exceeds ten pounds | It is unobjectionable provided the act is not detected
exam: A child walks a dog five times weekly at one pound per walk. Determine the total earnings over four weeks. | Twenty pounds | Nine pounds | Five pounds | Twenty-five pounds
